    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d133">

                <pb facs="0231" n="151" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg151"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A3/1886a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-11-05T16:00:00-05:00">November 5, 1936—4
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>139. You are requested to present to the Japanese Government a memorandum stating
                    that this Government has decided to exercise its right under Article 17 of the
                    London Naval Treaty, 1930, to retain 19,000 tons of destroyers in view of the
                    fact that on December 31, 1936, we will have only 75,900 tons of completed
                    cruisers of sub-category (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>), that is to say 67,600
                    less than the tonnage which is permitted under the terms of the 1930 Naval
                    Treaty in this sub-category.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
